+<section class="fork-gap-section" aria-labelledby="audit-method">
+<section class="fork-gap-section" aria-labelledby="oct19-projects">
+<h2 id="oct19-projects">New project comparisons &mdash; PSXCore, DS4RTBT and macOS tools</h2>
+<h3>PSXCore (PX1–PX10) &mdash; POPStarter and Ember parity</h3>
+<p><b>Pinned revisions:</b> <a href="https://github.com/dnunezx/LUNA/commit/4614b0c53e0f904a57afb18572b15090a6afd657">LUNA@4614b0c5</a> still pins <a href="https://github.com/dnunezx/PSXCore/tree/360a788ff7bb7c6853702a8edec6860cae49c0a2">PSXCore@360a788f</a>. Public PSXCore moved one commit to <a href="https://github.com/dnunezx/PSXCore/commit/66c70e9f920c39ebbe8c3d1deb7cfc7a78cf4fc9">66c70e9f</a> (bounded unaligned upload-copy code), <em>not</em> yet integrated into LUNA. Since the last fork-gap PR update, LUNA itself changed README text only; this section audits features previously underclassified from its October 17–19 work.</p>
+<p><b>Do not confuse runtime architectures.</b> <a href="https://github.com/NathanNeurotic/Open-PS2-Loader/blob/rebuild/main/docs/VCD.md">RiptOPL already launches PS1 VCDs via POPStarter</a>, which uses Sony POPS. <a href="https://github.com/dnunezx/PSXCore">PSXCore</a> likewise adapts the user-supplied Sony POPS executable in memory; it is not an independent emulator. <a href="https://github.com/Gageformer/Ember/blob/main/README.md">Ember</a> implements its own PS1 emulation from CUE/BIN data. Internal POPS patches cannot be transferred blindly to Ember, and without matched game/hardware tests a different source implementation is not proof of better compatibility.</p>
+<table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Precisely tracked source difference</th><th>Existing RiptOPL capability / limitations</th></tr></thead><tbody>
+<tr><td><code>PX1</code></td><td>Optional decoded-code validation with a LUNA per-game control. Public tester defaults OFF.</td><td>No matching PSXCore toggle in POPStarter/Ember UI. Test a known non-booting title before claiming improvement. <a href="https://github.com/dnunezx/PSXCore/blob/66c70e9f920c39ebbe8c3d1deb7cfc7a78cf4fc9/src/compat.c">Compatibility resolver</a>.</td></tr>
+<tr><td><code>PX2</code></td><td>GPU-information query correction; texture-window wrapping; valid GPU DMA from PS1 RAM address zero; CLUT refresh; GTE register/colour fixes.</td><td>Both existing cores already render PS1 graphics. These are granular correctness candidates rather than missing graphics support. <a href="https://github.com/dnunezx/PSXCore/blob/66c70e9f920c39ebbe8c3d1deb7cfc7a78cf4fc9/src/runtime_corrections.c">Expected-byte patch references</a>.</td></tr>
+<tr><td><code>PX3</code></td><td>Reusable, source-buildable, guarded POPS adapter and separate PS2 tester.</td><td>An alternative to bundled POPStarter; generic PS1 launching is already delivered. <a href="https://github.com/dnunezx/PSXCore/blob/66c70e9f920c39ebbe8c3d1deb7cfc7a78cf4fc9/INTEGRATION.md">Integration API</a>.</td></tr>
+<tr><td><code>PX4</code></td><td>Optional third PS1 launch core, selectable per game and with a bounded ELF/bootstrap handoff.</td><td><b>Frontend gap:</b> RiptOPL's VCD rows use POPStarter and its CUE/BIN folders use Ember. PSXCore would require a distinct per-title choice, settings migration, preflight verification, fallback and device capabilities. <a href="https://github.com/NathanNeurotic/Open-PS2-Loader/blob/rebuild/main/docs/VCD.md">Current two-core behavior</a>.</td></tr>
+<tr><td><code>PX5</code></td><td>MDEC fast paths, RGB24 movie scanout, buffered interlace, raster and video-transition corrections.</td><td>Ember and POPStarter already provide FMV playback and display modes; compare movie correctness and timing on specific discs. No cross-runtime test evidence yet. <a href="https://github.com/dnunezx/PSXCore/blob/66c70e9f920c39ebbe8c3d1deb7cfc7a78cf4fc9/runtime/Makefile">Source modules/flags</a>.</td></tr>
+<tr><td><code>PX6</code></td><td>CD-ROM Play/SetMode audio-sector correction, SPU state preservation, capture IRQ, CDDA/reverb and PCM gap handling.</td><td>Existing cores already play PS1 audio; this is a per-game audio fidelity investigation, not a missing sound system. <a href="https://github.com/dnunezx/PSXCore/blob/66c70e9f920c39ebbe8c3d1deb7cfc7a78cf4fc9/src/runtime_corrections.c">CDDA/SPU patch</a> · <a href="https://github.com/dnunezx/PSXCore/blob/66c70e9f920c39ebbe8c3d1deb7cfc7a78cf4fc9/runtime/Makefile">Reverb/MDEC modules</a>.</td></tr>
+<tr><td><code>PX7</code></td><td>Guarded guest memory access, load/return delay behavior, scratchpad handling and loop-cycle scheduling.</td><td>Compare CPU-sensitive games and representative scenes. Source changes in POPS are not a direct Ember implementation recipe. <a href="https://github.com/dnunezx/PSXCore/blob/66c70e9f920c39ebbe8c3d1deb7cfc7a78cf4fc9/README.md">PSXCore correction summary</a>.</td></tr>
+<tr><td><code>PX8</code></td><td>Checksum-verified POPS/runtime identity with bounded expected-byte, memory-layout and transactional patch checks.</td><td>Safety candidate for a third core; don't assume undocumented POPStarter internals lack such safeguards. <a href="https://github.com/dnunezx/PSXCore/blob/66c70e9f920c39ebbe8c3d1deb7cfc7a78cf4fc9/src/patch.c">Transactional patch code</a>.</td></tr>
+<tr><td><code>PX9</code></td><td>Native PS1 save-worker guards for paired-card readiness, precise writes, backup flush, rename and worker synchronization.</td><td>POPStarter and Ember already save PS1 games. This is PSXCore's experimental write lifecycle, not a universal safe-save capability. The project admits no proven power-loss durability or save-aware IGR shutdown. <a href="https://github.com/dnunezx/PSXCore/blob/66c70e9f920c39ebbe8c3d1deb7cfc7a78cf4fc9/INTEGRATION.md">Limitations</a>.</td></tr>
+<tr><td><code>PX10</code></td><td>Two-to-four-disc identity validation: header/TOC hashes, exact sectors, consistent save binding and bounded disc paths.</td><td>Complements LU12's LUNA playlist UI; more precise than filename-only grouping. Do not claim that the PSXCore <em>tester</em> can swap discs: it currently disables swap. <a href="https://github.com/dnunezx/PSXCore/blob/66c70e9f920c39ebbe8c3d1deb7cfc7a78cf4fc9/src/discset.c">Validator code</a>.</td></tr>
+</tbody></table>
+<h4>What is already present versus genuinely new</h4>
+<table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>RiptOPL POPStarter</th><th>RiptOPL Ember</th><th>LUNA / PSXCore</th></tr></thead><tbody>
+<tr><td>PS1 launch</td><td>VCD on multiple local/remote backends</td><td>CUE/BIN game folders on supported backends</td><td>VCD runtime, LUNA-integrated option; standalone tester only USB and internal FAT32/exFAT</td></tr>
+<tr><td>PS1 cards</td><td>Existing POPS/VMC and BDMA paths</td><td>Auto per-game cards and optional <code>SharedMC.txt</code> aliases</td><td>Shared/per-game 128 KiB cards; LUNA named pairs, backup and restore; real-console persistence unproven</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Sound / video</td><td>Sony POPS audio, graphics and FMVs</td><td>Independent emulator with PAL timing, video modes, dither and audio options</td><td>Specific POPS audio/graphics corrections; no independent compatibility victory established</td></tr>
+<tr><td>Disc sets</td><td>POPStarter-specific swap/reset methods</td><td>Upstream disc-file selection; no equivalent integrated IGR disc swap proven</td><td>LUNA playlist grouping + separately enabled runtime swapping; standalone tester disables it</td></tr>
+<tr><td>In-game return</td><td>POPStarter's own reset semantics</td><td>No Ember IGR in the RiptOPL docs</td><td>LUNA PSXCore Quit/Play menu when compiled and installed for that path; public tester has it disabled</td></tr>
+</tbody></table>
+<h4>Experimental and incomplete capabilities (also inventoried)</h4>
+<p><a href="https://github.com/dnunezx/PSXCore/blob/66c70e9f920c39ebbe8c3d1deb7cfc7a78cf4fc9/DISC-MAPPING.md">Prepared native APA/PFS mapping</a> is an experimental API and does not make an APA game browser/loader; RiptOPL's current POPStarter already has APA support. The <a href="https://github.com/dnunezx/PSXCore/blob/66c70e9f920c39ebbe8c3d1deb7cfc7a78cf4fc9/src/compat_profiles.generated.h">published automatic compatibility profile catalog has zero entries</a>. Adaptive Audio is OFF by default in the tester and may affect tempo/pitch. MX4SIO/MMCE and multiple USB support are not proven in the standalone tester. PSXCore's tester release has <b>no established physical-console gameplay or save/reload validation</b>, and IGR/disc swapping are disabled there, despite LUNA's separately configured runtime.</p>
+<h4>Validation and safe implementation scope</h4>
+<p>Preserve POPStarter and Ember as default choices. Any experimental PSXCore integration must verify user-provided POPS file checksums, patch bytes, ELF layout, per-title storage and memory-card ownership before IOP handoff. Compare identical legal disc data across all <em>supported</em> runtimes for startup, GPU/GTE effects, MDEC/FMVs, SPU/reverb/CDDA, controller behavior, disc swap, native saves after full reboot and IGR return. Label source presence, host-test success and actual hardware outcomes independently. No automatic migration, overwritten memory cards, blanket POPS patches, exclusive device claims or premature release.</p>
+<h3>OPL-DS4RTBT (DT1–DT3)</h3>
+<p><a href="https://github.com/MiguelQueiroz010/OPL-DS4RTBT/tree/rtl-fw-loader">RTL-enabled Bluetooth fork</a> adds Realtek firmware handling (DT1), DS4 clone HID output/CRC and rumble (DT2), and four-pad menu status plus controller setup/test (DT3). These extend <a href="#dualsense-bt">DB1–DB2</a> (original DualSense BT/pairing), and are fully scoped in <a href="https://github.com/NathanNeurotic/Open-PS2-Loader/issues/972">issue #972</a>. Distinguish four <em>menu</em> pads from in-game multitap. Firmware licensing and real hardware remain verification gates.</p>
+<h3>PS2 Manager for macOS (MA1–MA2)</h3>
+<p><a href="https://github.com/Gionnio/ps2manager">Native macOS manager</a> supports PS2 ISO imports and PS1 CUE/BIN-to-VCD conversion with POPStarter multi-disc package sidecars (MA1), and per-game user-supplied POPS patch archives plus VCD reverse conversion (MA2). These are desktop-library workflow candidates for PS2-Servers and external utilities rather than missing OPL game-core functions.</p>
+</section>
