+        {
+          "sha": "70841538868a25be88a802120f61ea48dc02498b",
+          "message": "VMC: opt-in Rebuild for a card too fragmented for the native core (#986)\n\nAn existing card that holds saves but is in too many pieces for native\nmcemu (BDM: more than one extent; HDD: PFS map too long) was skipped at\nevery launch with no way to fix it from the loader. The per-game VMC\ndialog now offers Rebuild for exactly that case, after confirmation:\n\n- genvmc gets a copy mode (VMC_CREATE_REBUILD): it creates <name>.new\n  exclusively with the same reservation a new card gets (BDM one extent\n  via VMC_ALLOC, HDD one PIOCALLOC, refusal = no room), copies every\n  byte, reads both back and compares, and on BDM confirms the extent.\n  It only accepts a .new target and derives the .bin source itself, so\n  it can never write or remove a card; the card is opened read-only.\n- The EE (src/vmcrebuild.c) proves .bak/.new absent by reading the\n  folder (PFS rename replaces its target), runs the launch's own layout\n  check on the copy, then renames card -> .bak and copy -> .bin, undoing\n  the first rename if the second fails and syncing PFS after renames.\n  Only the copy this rebuild made is ever removed, and only while the\n  card is confirmed in place. Card and backup are never deleted.\n\nHost tests: test_vmc_rebuild.py drives the real genvmc and EE code over\nan in-memory fs with a fault at every step on BDM and PFS (mutation-\nchecked); test_vmc_rebuild_fatfs.py fragments a real card on FAT32 and\nexFAT images, gets \"no room\" with nothing changed, then rebuilds it to\none extent with identical bytes and the backup kept across remount.\nZERO hardware testing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>"
+        },
+        {
+          "sha": "d0f2c6500e400d2d7d3971becbaf9feb1c4312d9",
+          "message": "vmcrebuild: include ioman.h for LOG and opt in to fileXio under newlib\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>"
+        },
+        {
+          "sha": "21a31e638ceb3393e07d248216e3a645753069ed",
+          "message": "VMC rebuild: offer only at sizes Rebuild accepts; keep the unknown-flag test on an unused bit\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>"
+        },
+        {
+          "sha": "6f7c7cdd60f74e983d274a50e81ddc6186c2d66e",
+          "message": "Add compat Mode 8 Slow Reads: Accurate Reads at the CD rate for DVD (#924)\n\nTales of the Abyss loses its music and SFX after Shurrey Hill and corrupts\nSFX boarding the flying ship. Testers on the psx-place OPL thread found it\nplays clean at <=1000KB/s and breaks at >=1200KB/s; OPL 0.9's Mode 1\n(~900KB/s) fixed it. Our Mode 1 throttles DVD media to 33512 ticks/sector\n(~2200KB/s), so it stays too fast.\n\nHonouring the game's requested spindle speed was ruled out: nothing in our\ncode shows what this title asks for, and changing how sceCdRead's mode is\nread would change the read rate of every Mode 1 game that passes a\nnon-maximum speed.\n\nMode 8 (OPL core only, greyed under Neutrino) turns Accurate Reads on and\nreads DVD media at the CD rate (81920 ticks/sector, ~900KB/s). Mode 1 alone\nkeeps CD 81920 / DVD 33512, so no other game changes speed. No title\ndefault is added until the reporter confirms on hardware.\n\nCOMPAT_MODE_COUNT goes 6 -> 8 so the per-game screen reads and saves bit 7.\nThat also makes the existing Mode 7 checkbox (Neutrino -gc=7) save: it has\nbeen drawn since rebuild-08 but its bit was dropped on save.\n\nHost test test_accurate_read_rate.py compiles the real rate function and\npins the ticks/sector for CD/DVD with and without Mode 8.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>"
