+          "sha": "582f562fc2791d7512c14214cb69badd4003eb84",
+          "message": "fix(udpfs): deliver the last byte of odd-sized reads (#1010)\n\nsmap_fifo_read() copied the PIO tail of a received frame as whole words\nand then one u16, with no step for a single last byte. Every odd-length\nreply -- the final packet of every odd-sized file read over UDPFS --\ncame back at the right size with its last byte left as whatever the\nbuffer held before.\n\nThe #1010 tester's Neutrino folder on mc1 shows exactly that: all 29\ndamaged files have an odd size and differ only in their last byte, and\nno even-sized file is touched. The NUL that ended i_dev9_hidden.toml\nstops Neutrino with \"bad control char in comment\" (his udptty log; the\nsame files fail the same way in Neutrino's own tomlc17 on a PC), so the\nUDPFS driver never loads and every game resets to the browser. In\nRiptOPL's own menu the lost byte breaks the IEND CRC of odd-sized PNG\ncovers read over UDPFS.\n\nRead the tail as one whole word (the FIFO pads each frame to 4 bytes)\nand store only its valid bytes. Upstream Neutrino master has the same\ntail. test_udpfs_smap_fifo_tail.py compiles the real function against a\nsimulated FIFO and fails on the old code for every odd byte count.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>"
+        },
+        {
+          "sha": "da4eca8a5a7d1de626228a1df30ed0530b9f4682",
+          "message": "fix(lang): lock the language registry; show English for unsafe translations\n\nAddress the CodeRabbit review of #1017.\n\n1. The registry is now a growable array whose entries can be replaced,\n   and the IO worker changed it with no lock while the GUI thread read\n   it. lngReadEntry grows, fills and replaces a path only under the GUI\n   lock (old path freed after), nLanguages is published under it, and\n   lngSetGuiValue copies the path under it and reads the file with the\n   lock released. Every other reader runs on the IO worker or inside a\n   locked GUI frame. The discovery and switch tests now fail on an\n   unlocked realloc or a nested lock.\n\n2. The audit checked printf tokens on the fork overlay only, though the\n   upstream string wins. Run against upstream today, ten upstream\n   strings differ from English, and several would crash: French\n   AUTO_START_IN_N_SECS has %s for an int (the Auto Start countdown),\n   and three Persian strings swap or add a %s. lngLoadFromFile now\n   checks every loaded string (lngFormatFits) and uses the English one\n   when its conversions would read other argument types; the same ones\n   or fewer in order are kept. The audit checks the merged strings: an\n   overlay mismatch fails --strict, an upstream one is a warning, so an\n   upstream change cannot break the language build.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>"
