+          "message": "Build 104: Arabic and Persian drawn right to left, letters joined\n\nRIPPS2 draws text glyph by glyph from the left with no shaping engine, so\nArabic and Persian showed their letters unjoined and backwards. fntsys now\nhands any string with right-to-left letters to ripps2Rtl (ripps2tr.c): each\nArabic letter becomes the presentation form its neighbours call for (lam with\nalef as one ligature, ZWNJ stopping a join, vowel marks passed over), and\neach line is laid out right to left, Latin words and numbers (with their %,\n. and :) kept in reading order and brackets turned round. Text is measured\nfrom the same joined letters and wrapped before it is turned, so wrapped\nhints read top to bottom.\n\nA mark with no advance that its font draws right of the pen (DejaVu's Arabic\nvowel marks, Noto Sans Lao's vowels and tone marks: placed by OpenType, which\nfntsys does not read) is now centred over the letter before it, a second mark\nabove stacking over the first.\n\nThe pack carries RIPPS2's own font for Arabic and Persian (DejaVu Sans 2.37\ncut to Latin-1, the Arabic block and both presentation-form blocks: upstream's\ncut had none of the joined forms) and Noto Sans Lao 2.003 for Laotian, each\nwith its licence text (elf/lang/fonts, make_lang_fonts.py). Upstream's older\nPersian lines were stored pre-joined in an order made for another renderer;\nthe pack leaves those 248 out and Persian.yml has them written plain.\n\nThe joining table is generated from Unicode's data (make_arabic_forms.py).\nThe build 104 host test checks ripps2Rtl against vectors from a Python twin,\nand Lao line breaks (never before a vowel or tone mark of the letter before,\nnor after a vowel written ahead of its letter).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
