+  <thead><tr><th>Capability</th><th>Caduceus + Caduceus OPL</th><th>PS2-Servers + RiptOPL</th><th>Gap / interpretation for us</th></tr></thead>
+  <tbody>
+    <tr><td><b>Game-serving protocols</b></td><td>Bundles <a href="https://github.com/elmariolo/OPL-Server">OPLServer</a> for SMB game shares. Caduceus documents a default <code>PS2</code> share, TCP <code>1024</code>.</td><td>PS2-Servers Desktop/Core support SMBv1, SMB2/3, UDPFS, UDPBD and experimental HTTP; Edge covers SMBv1, UDPFS and UDPBD.</td><td><b>Advantage: ours.</b> The server's protocol support does not automatically make a console loader support the same protocol. <a href="https://github.com/NathanNeurotic/PS2-Servers/blob/c1eb1e5aecd9e8359dc5b7799fc7f135a81f51d5/README.md">PS2-Servers modes</a>.</td></tr>
+    <tr><td><b>Desktop game library and ISO management</b></td><td>SQLite catalogue, cover images and details, local ISO import, installed-game list, download-link transfer tracking and JSON/SQLite catalogue import/backup. <a href="https://github.com/Rian6/caduceus/blob/311f4eabc4ddaad649cbd0d60859f27d0ffcbbfc/README.md">Host documentation</a>.</td><td>PS2-Servers selects serving roots, checks access, configures protocols and logs traffic; no comparable curated SQLite game catalogue is documented.</td><td><b>Host companion gap.</b> Optional catalogue + import/cover manager could sit above existing server backends. A Caduceus catalogue backup explicitly does <em>not</em> contain ISOs, covers, VMC saves or settings.</td></tr>
+    <tr><td><b>Current-game activity and Discord</b></td><td>Displays the detected running title and cover on PC; Discord Rich Presence can include title, cover and latest unlocked achievement. <a href="https://github.com/Rian6/caduceus/blob/311f4eabc4ddaad649cbd0d60859f27d0ffcbbfc/electron/discord-presence.ts">Presence integration</a>.</td><td>PS2-Servers exposes service status, logs and diagnostics, not a documented game-aware desktop activity/presence layer. RiptOPL's separate Discord launch-announcement proposal is already U6.</td><td><b>Host companion opportunity</b> for optional session reporting. Console and server activity are not the same proof of a successfully launched game; do not count U6 again.</td></tr>
+    <tr><td><b>PC RetroAchievements experience</b></td><td>Integrates a modified <a href="https://github.com/Rian6/caduceus/blob/311f4eabc4ddaad649cbd0d60859f27d0ffcbbfc/vendor/xerabora/README.md">xeRAbora alpha.12</a>, account progress browser, live events, sound and protected Windows token storage.</td><td>RiptOPL already supports xeRAbora telemetry and in-game unlocks, with <a href="https://github.com/NathanNeurotic/Open-PS2-Loader/blob/7452341a076d3e7a5e14e613429c7eca594f4888/docs/RETROACHIEVEMENTS.md">alpha.15-aligned integration</a>; PS2-Servers does not document an integrated RA account UI or controller.</td><td><b>Host companion gap.</b> Consider an optional RA manager/UI; retain current RA protocol and client compatibility rather than replacing them with an older pinned adapter.</td></tr>
+    <tr><td><b>Achievements browsed on the PS2</b></td><td>Caduceus OPL adds an account games/achievements category, points, icons, earned/locked/hardcore filters and pagination. It requests data over UDP <code>18198</code> using a capability stored on the private SMB share at <code>ART/CADUCEUS.KEY</code>. <a href="https://github.com/Rian6/caduceus-opl/blob/cc1713a893a976c9b26ae1dd9975c200a461edfe/docs/protocol/PROTOCOL.md">Wire protocol</a>.</td><td>RiptOPL has support checks, list badges and in-game telemetry/overlay, but no equivalent integrated RA account-progress browser is documented. ORBIT already supplies gap <a href="#orbit">OR4</a>.</td><td><b>Real console UI opportunity, already counted.</b> A Caduceus OPL-only UDP service is not automatically available to RiptOPL; an explicit compatible host service and console client are needed.</td></tr>
+    <tr><td><b>In-game achievement pop-up</b></td><td>Displays an experimental notice with achievement name/points. The linked implementation still notes ongoing physical-console validation. <a href="https://github.com/Rian6/caduceus-opl/blob/cc1713a893a976c9b26ae1dd9975c200a461edfe/README.md">Console status</a>.</td><td>RiptOPL's unlock overlay already displayed an achievement on physical hardware in its recorded MMCE/USB tests; other routes remain unverified.</td><td><b>Overlap, not a new gap.</b> Different visuals or message handling do not establish better launch compatibility.</td></tr>
+    <tr><td><b>UI and onboarding</b></td><td>Windows installer, first-run setup guide, XMB-style console UI, details card and art conversion into <code>ART/&lt;Game ID&gt;_COV.png</code>.</td><td>PS2-Servers has cross-platform Desktop/Core/Edge and protocol-oriented setup tools. RiptOPL already has its own themes, artwork naming, launcher UI and device support.</td><td><b>Design/integration opportunity, not a missing loading feature.</b> Reuse compatible interaction ideas, not hard-coded Caduceus artwork paths: RiptOPL also has filename-based VCD, ELF and Ember-folder identities. <a href="https://github.com/Rian6/caduceus-opl/blob/cc1713a893a976c9b26ae1dd9975c200a461edfe/docs/OPL-XMB.md">XMB/art reference</a>.</td></tr>
+    <tr><td><b>Portability and diagnostics</b></td><td>Caduceus PC app is Windows-focused. Its paired OPL client and host must cooperate for integrated covers/achievements.</td><td>PS2-Servers Desktop works on Windows, Linux and macOS; Core runs headlessly; Go Edge targets NAS, routers and embedded Linux, with diagnostic and auto-negotiation options.</td><td><b>Advantage: ours.</b> Keep protocol engines, headless portability, per-peer UDPFS handling and safety checks decoupled from any optional catalogue/RA frontend. <a href="https://github.com/NathanNeurotic/PS2-Servers/blob/c1eb1e5aecd9e8359dc5b7799fc7f135a81f51d5/docs/EDITIONS.md">Editions</a>.</td></tr>
+  </tbody>
+</table>
+<h3>Compatibility and implementation boundaries</h3>
+<p>Do <b>not</b> assume the two ecosystems can be swapped by changing the server address. Caduceus OPL expects the paired <code>PS2</code> SMB share (normally port <code>1024</code>), plus custom RA catalogue/session and account APIs on UDP <code>18197</code>/<code>18198</code>. PS2-Servers Desktop normally advertises SMBv1 share <code>games</code> on port <code>1025</code> (Core/Edge default to <code>1111</code>). Share name, port, root layout, credentials, services and console configuration must match. Two services cannot both bind the same host address, protocol and port. Caduceus' custom achievement browser needs a compatible server component; serving the same ISO files via PS2-Servers does not supply it.</p>
+<p>Both ecosystems inherit the upstream warning that <b>RA telemetry during SMB game streaming can disrupt loads</b>; neither pairing demonstrates a fix for that issue. Keep host tokens and the Caduceus read-capability key private; UDP catalogue traffic is not encrypted. Caduceus PC sources currently have no top-level declared GitHub repository licence, so obtain clear reuse permission or review licensing before copying original application/bridge code. The inherited OPL/xeRAbora components have their own licences and notices.</p>
+<h3>Integration priorities for our pairing</h3>
+<ol>
+<li><b>Optional PC library module:</b> add metadata/catalogue, cover preparation, installed-game inventory and non-destructive backup as a frontend/sidecar to PS2-Servers; keep existing service implementations unchanged. Account for RiptOPL's disc-ID PS2 art and filename/folder-based VCD, ELF and Ember art identities.</li>
+<li><b>Read-only game-session reporting:</b> surface the running title and its image/cover to an optional desktop view; only add Discord presence if the session identity is dependable. Avoid injecting traffic or polling into the hot UDPFS/SMB transfer paths.</li>
+<li><b>Optional RA account viewer:</b> build on the compatible xeRAbora client already used by RiptOPL, with secrets held on the host, bounded requests and a separately tested console-side browser. Do not introduce a competing telemetry client or incompatible protocol by default; OR4/CO3 are now implemented; use <a href="retroachievements.html">the RA guide</a> for current browser support and validation limits.</li>
+<li><b>Integration tests first:</b> verify share/port selection, art naming, concurrent listeners, offline fallback, IOP resets, real-PS2 UI memory limits and performance before treating the pairing as interchangeable. Preserve existing servers and saved libraries during any migration.</li>
+</ol>
+
+
+<h2 id="pc-card-cf">PS2CF — CompactFlash over the original PC CARD slot</h2>
+<p><a href="https://github.com/mihhru/PS2CF">mihhru/PS2CF 1.0</a> (2026-10-14; <a href="https://github.com/mihhru/PS2CF/blob/main/VALIDATION.md">validation record</a>) adds a <em>different hardware storage bus</em>, not merely another FAT32/exFAT formatting option. SCPH-10000 has hardware evidence; SCPH-15000 and SCPH-18000 are targets awaiting separate confirmation. CF/ATA adapter compatibility is hardware dependent. RiptOPL's implementation was merged in <a href="https://github.com/NathanNeurotic/Open-PS2-Loader/pull/955">PR #955</a>. CF1–CF3 therefore describe integrated capabilities awaiting real-console validation, not absent implementation. See <a href="pc-card-cf.html">the current CF guide</a>. Related Launchstation file-manager work is tracked separately in <a href="https://github.com/NathanNeurotic/Launchstation/pull/156">#156</a>; its status does not establish a RiptOPL hardware result.</p>
+<table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Feature</th><th>Gap and integration boundary</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody>
+<tr><td><code>CF1</code></td><td><b>16-bit PC CARD CF/ATA hardware backend</b></td><td>PS2CF detects a retail PCMCIA DEV9 revision (<code>REV &amp; 0xF0 == 0x20</code>), checks controller/card state, reads CF CIS, selects a validated PIO profile and identifies ATA storage. RiptOPL now includes this distinct transport, with exclusive PC CARD ownership and runtime checks; hardware acceptance is pending. ROMVER/model must only be a hint; a PC CARD LAN adapter, empty slot, or owned DEV9 bus must never be seized.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/mihhru/PS2CF/blob/9b6ed3702b59a87512426952e003125a5b4f0e3c/src/opl/modules/pccf/pccf_hw.c">pccf_hw.c</a></td></tr>
+<tr><td><code>CF2</code></td><td><b>FAT32/exFAT CF game inventory</b></td><td>Enumerates PS2 ISOs from CF/SD-to-CF via the PC CARD slot, including single files larger than 4 GiB on exFAT. This is not identical to the already supported internal exFAT HDD or USB path. RiptOPL retains its own ART/CFG and image-title naming in the integrated backend.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/mihhru/PS2CF/blob/9b6ed3702b59a87512426952e003125a5b4f0e3c/src/opl/modules/pccf/main.c">CF block-device registration</a> · <a href="https://github.com/mihhru/PS2CF/blob/main/README.md">hardware/ISO examples</a></td></tr>
+<tr><td><code>CF3</code></td><td><b>Dedicated read-only in-game CF/CDVD driver</b></td><td>The menu and in-game IOP drivers are distinct (<code>pccf_bd</code>, <code>pccf_game</code>, <code>pccf_cdvdman</code>). RiptOPL integrates both menu and read-only game paths; their console lifecycle remains unverified. CF-hosted OPL VMC, simultaneous PC CARD LAN and CF, and CF+network achievement telemetry are <b>not</b> claimed supported by PS2CF 1.0.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/mihhru/PS2CF/blob/9b6ed3702b59a87512426952e003125a5b4f0e3c/src/opl/Makefile">module build list</a> · <a href="https://github.com/mihhru/PS2CF/blob/main/CHANGES.md">limitations</a></td></tr>
+<tr><td><code>CF4</code></td><td><b>Companion wLaunchELF <code>cf:/</code> file operations</b> <span class="pill">Companion</span></td><td>PS2CF's wLaunchELF adds CF browsing, copy, rename, create and delete on FAT32/exFAT. This is a <em>Launchstation file-manager gap</em>, not proof RiptOPL needs to become a file manager. Cross-project tracking: <a href="https://github.com/NathanNeurotic/Launchstation/pull/156">Launchstation #156</a>. Direct ELF launch from <code>cf:/</code> remains unsupported upstream.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/mihhru/PS2CF/blob/main/src/wlaunchelf/iop/pccf/main.c">wLaunchELF CF IOP module</a></td></tr>
+</tbody></table>
+<p><b>Integration safety:</b> PS2CF disables competing network/HDD paths in its dedicated build. That blanket policy cannot be transplanted into RiptOPL. The new backend must own the PC CARD bus only when safe, fail closed on conflicts, preserve all other game devices, and not alter users' existing firmware or memory-card images. The <a href="https://github.com/NathanNeurotic/Open-PS2-Loader/blob/rebuild/main/docs/PC-CARD-CF.md">current implementation reference</a> defines ownership and remaining validation.</p>
+
+<h2 id="dualsense-bt">OPL-DualSense-BT (October 2026)</h2>
+<p><a href="https://github.com/rodrigoGalvanDev/OPL-DualSense-BT">rodrigoGalvanDev/OPL-DualSense-BT</a> is a new OPL fork with real-console testing of a DualSense and one Realtek Bluetooth adapter on a SCPH-90001. <a href="https://github.com/MiguelQueiroz010/OPL-DS4RTBT">MiguelQueiroz010/OPL-DS4RTBT</a> is a recent downstream fork; its initial README mirrors the original and is <b>not</b> counted as a separate source of independently verified capabilities. This is different from the historic U9 poll-delay experiment and from existing wired DualSense handling.</p>
+<table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Feature</th><th>Gap and limitations</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody>
+<tr><td><code>DB1</code></td><td><b>DualSense over Bluetooth in menu and game</b></td><td>Implements DualSense input/output reports, output CRC, SDP responses and in-game PADEMU operation. The fork reports input, vibration and a mixed wired-controller test on one console/dongle. Compare its menu driver and in-game driver independently to RiptOPL before porting; USB and Bluetooth PADEMU share limited IOP resources.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/rodrigoGalvanDev/OPL-DualSense-BT/blob/dualsense-bt/modules/ds34bt/iop/ds34bt.c">menu BT driver</a> · <a href="https://github.com/rodrigoGalvanDev/OPL-DualSense-BT/blob/dualsense-bt/modules/pademu/ds34bt.c">in-game BT</a></td></tr>
+<tr><td><code>DB2</code></td><td><b>Wireless discovery and Secure Simple Pairing UI</b></td><td>Scans nearby pads and pairs DS4/DualSense using a GUI <code>Search</code> action and saved link keys without requiring prior USB pairing. Actual hardware confirmation is limited to the stated DualSense/adapter; DS4 discovery, other dongles and multiple wireless controllers need validation. Never overwrite existing paired-key storage without migration.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/rodrigoGalvanDev/OPL-DualSense-BT/blob/dualsense-bt/include/btkeys.h">btkeys.h</a> · <a href="https://github.com/rodrigoGalvanDev/OPL-DualSense-BT/blob/dualsense-bt/src/dialogs.c">pairing UI</a></td></tr>
